1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,660
Most objection handling frameworks are

2
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:02,550
built on instinct.

3
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,330
AI can build them on evidence.

4
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:07,190
The traditional approach to objection

5
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:09,040
handling is to collect the most common

6
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:11,770
objections, write responses, and train

7
00:00:11,890 --> 00:00:12,830
reps to deliver them.

8
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:15,080
The problem is that the responses are

9
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:16,730
based on what experienced reps think

10
00:00:16,790 --> 00:00:19,210
works, not on what actually moves deals

11
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:19,660
forward.

12
00:00:20,410 --> 00:00:21,400
AI changes that.

13
00:00:22,050 --> 00:00:24,070
It can analyze thousands of recorded

14
00:00:24,110 --> 00:00:26,120
calls and identify which responses to

15
00:00:26,190 --> 00:00:28,640
specific objections correlate with deals

16
00:00:28,690 --> 00:00:29,930
advancing versus stalling.

17
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:32,290
It can show you that the response your

18
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:33,760
team has been training for the price

19
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:36,130
objection actually increases churn risk.

20
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:38,590
It can identify which objections are

21
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:40,820
real blockers and which ones are just

22
00:00:40,990 --> 00:00:42,540
friction that better discovery would

23
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:43,110
have prevented.

24
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:46,310
The framework stops being a best-guess

25
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:48,660
document and becomes a data-driven

26
00:00:48,710 --> 00:00:50,580
playbook that updates as the market

27
00:00:50,610 --> 00:00:51,090
changes.

28
00:00:52,050 --> 00:00:54,020
Reps stop handling objections based on

29
00:00:54,070 --> 00:00:56,300
what sounds right and start handling

30
00:00:56,350 --> 00:00:57,880
them based on what actually works.

31
00:00:58,170 --> 00:01:00,330
That is not a marginal improvement.

32
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:03,200
That is a structural advantage in every

33
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:04,070
competitive deal.

34
00:01:04,510 --> 00:01:07,880
Follow A-Gent for the agent-first GTM

35
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:08,370
playbook.
